  "summary": "Recent preprints pair longitudinal lab series, gene annotations, and trial and ChEMBL records with LLM or network inference for disease-level analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is inference anchored in structured biomedical resources rather than raw free text. One preprint converts longitudinal blood-test time series into correlation-based causal disease networks, with thresholding strategies governing which edges are retained. A second applies an LLM to abstracts to predict gene annotations, building gene-based similarity across medical literature that links behavioral and molecular research. A third uses an LLM-assisted pipeline to assemble, audit, and prioritize therapeutic assets per disease from trial records and ChEMBL, demonstrated in three diseases. Where LLMs appear, text is the primary input; where time series or curated records appear, they supply the inferential scaffold. What is new is how each domain's structure enters the pipeline: laboratory trajectories, gene annotation similarity, and trial and ChEMBL records. All three items are recent preprints, so their findings should be read as provisional.",
